    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Mickey Moniak** headlines our lineup with an elite 75.98 score, capitalizing on a favorable clash against Roki Sasaki. His projection is bolstered by a significant 14% boost from a prime platoon advantage and a 14% lift from a hitter-friendly park; notably, Sasaki\u2019s slow delivery provides a unique opportunity for **Mickey Moniak** to exploit the basepaths, a critical factor given our prioritization of the scarcest category, SB. **Isaac Paredes** (64.31) and **Jose Caballero** (64.59) also grade out as strong plays. **Jose Caballero** benefits immensely from a 19% boost due to the matchup against Chris Bassitt, and despite a late-order penalty, his athleticism makes him a high-upside asset. Meanwhile, **Josh Naylor** (60.48) remains a reliable anchor in the utility spot, benefiting from a favorable platoon split against the right-handed Dustin May.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved **Austin Riley**, who narrowly missed the starting cut with a 54.15 score. While **Austin Riley** offers power and benefits from the potential to swipe bags against a slow-working pitcher, the optimizer favored the higher efficiency scores of **Max Muncy** (56.11) and **Corbin Carroll** (56.41). Despite **Max Muncy** facing a tough challenge in Seth Lugo, his base projection held strong enough to edge out the bench. We are keeping a close eye on **Brice Turang** and **Vinnie Pasquantino**; with lineups currently pending, their projected order placements are vital to these efficiency ratings. **Corbin Carroll** carries some risk due to a negative platoon split against Tolle, but his elite speed profile ensures he remains a must-start as we hunt for stolen bases to gain ground in the standings. **James Wood** remains on the shelf, leaving us to navigate these matchups without his high-ceiling production.",
